            <ArticleTitle>The association of elevated trough serum vancomycin concentrations with obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>507-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jiac.2015.03.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1341-321X(15)00072-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Obese patients display differences in vancomycin drug disposition, which may complicate attainment of appropriate serum vancomycin concentrations (SVCs). This study was conducted to determine if obesity leads to trough SVCs above the therapeutic range.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This retrospective cohort study sought to determine the rate and predictors of high (i.e. &gt;20 mg/L) serum trough levels according to level of obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Increasing BMI predicted SVCs &gt; 20 mg/L after controlling for dose, age, and serum creatinine. Obese patients had significantly higher mean trough SVCs compared to non-obese patients (16.5 mg/L vs 12.1 mg/L, p = 0.004) and a significantly higher proportion of obese patients had trough SVCs &gt; 20 mg/L (18.9% vs 4.2%, p = 0.03).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Increasing obesity predicted higher probabilities of SVCs &gt; 20 mg/L. Development of alternative dosing and management strategies for vancomycin may be necessary to account for pharmacokinetic changes associated with obesity.</AbstractText>
